# bean-there.imagine
# a language made of sentences. there is no grammar. write what you see.

imagine a page for a small coffee shop.
  the mood is: warm, unhurried, a little bit wooden.
  the background is the colour of oat milk, the text is the colour of espresso.

at the very top, a slim bar.
  on its left, in a friendly rounded font, the words "Bean There"
  on its right, three quiet links: "menu" · "hours" · "find us"
  the bar stays stuck to the top when the page scrolls.

below it, a tall opening scene, centered.
  a very large line of text: "we open at six."
  under it, smaller and softer: "the good beans go first."
  under that, [ see today's menu ] — a rounded button the colour of espresso.
  when someone clicks it, the page glides down to the menu.

then the menu.
  a heading: "today"
  three cards side by side, each with a thin border and generous whitespace:

    card one
      "flat white"
      "£3.20"
      "milk, folded not poured"

    card two
      "cortado"
      "£3.00"
      "small, serious, gone in four sips"

    card three
      "filter"
      "£2.60"
      "whatever the roaster is excited about this week"

  when the mouse hovers a card it lifts a few pixels and its shadow deepens.
  on a narrow screen the three cards stack into one column.

then the hours.
  a heading: "hours"
  "weekdays — 6am until the pastries run out"
  "weekends — 8am, slower, better"

then, at the very bottom, one small line, centered and faint:
  "made of beans and imagination"
